<h3 style='margin-top:16px;color:#1e1b4b;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd;padding-bottom:4px;'>Section A: Multiple Choice Questions</h3><p style='font-style:italic;color:#555;margin-bottom:8px;'>Note: Choose the correct option for each of the following questions.</p><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q1.</strong> Which of the following statements is TRUE about constructors in Java?</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) A constructor has the same name as the class and must specify a void return type.</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) A constructor has the same name as the class and does not have any return type, not even void.</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) A constructor can be invoked explicitly at any time using a method call.</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) A constructor is executed only when a class is imported.</li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q2.</strong> Which keyword is used to allocate memory and automatically invoke a constructor when creating an object?</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) class</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) import</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) new</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) this</li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q3.</strong> Consider the following class definition:
__CODE<sub>B</sub>LOCK<sub>0</sub>__
If an object is created as `Sample obj = new Sample();`, what will be the values of `obj.a` and `obj.b`?</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) a = 0, b = 0.0</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) a = 5, b = 10.5</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) a = 10, b = 5.0</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) Compilation error because no parameters were passed.</li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q4.</strong> What is the phenomenon of defining multiple constructors in the same class with different parameter lists called?</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) Constructor Overriding</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) Constructor Encapsulation</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) Constructor Overloading</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) Constructor Abstraction</li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q5.</strong> If no constructor is defined in a Java class, what does the Java compiler automatically insert?</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) Parameterized constructor</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) Copy constructor</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) Default constructor (no-argument constructor)</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) Static constructor</li></ul><h3 style='margin-top:16px;color:#1e1b4b;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd;padding-bottom:4px;'>Section B: Fill in the Blanks</h3><p style='font-style:italic;color:#555;margin-bottom:8px;'>Note: Fill in the blanks with appropriate terms.</p><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q1.</strong> A constructor that accepts arguments during object creation to initialize instance variables is known as a _______ constructor.</div><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q2.</strong> The default value initialized by Java's implicit default constructor for a `boolean` instance variable is _______.</div><h3 style='margin-top:16px;color:#1e1b4b;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd;padding-bottom:4px;'>Section C: Very Short Answer Questions</h3><p style='font-style:italic;color:#555;margin-bottom:8px;'>Note: Answer the following questions briefly.</p><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q1.</strong> State any two key differences between a Constructor and a Method in Java.</div><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q2.</strong> What is Constructor Overloading? State the condition necessary to differentiate overloaded constructors in a class.</div><h3 style='margin-top:16px;color:#1e1b4b;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd;padding-bottom:4px;'>Section D: Short Answer Questions</h3><p style='font-style:italic;color:#555;margin-bottom:8px;'>Note: Answer the following questions as directed.</p><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q1.</strong> Analyze the following Java code snippet and write its output:

__CODE<sub>B</sub>LOCK<sub>0</sub>__</div><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q2.</strong> Explain the purpose of the `this` keyword when instance variable names and constructor parameter names are identical. Write a brief code line to illustrate its usage.</div><h3 style='margin-top:16px;color:#1e1b4b;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd;padding-bottom:4px;'>Section E: Long Answer / Programming</h3><p style='font-style:italic;color:#555;margin-bottom:8px;'>Note: Write a complete Java program following the given specifications.</p><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q1.</strong> Design a class named `Bill` to calculate the total monthly telephone bill for a customer.

**Data Members / Instance Variables:**
- `int bno` : to store the bill number
- `String name` : to store the customer name
- `int calls` : to store the total number of calls made
- `double amount` : to store the total bill amount

**Member Methods:**
- `Bill()` : Default constructor to initialize integer data members to `0`, String to `""` (empty string), and double to `0.0`.
- `Bill(int b, String n, int c)` : Parameterized constructor to initialize `bno`, `name`, and `calls` with user-supplied values.
- `void calculate()` : To calculate the total bill amount based on the following call rates:
  - For the first 100 calls: Free
  - For the next 100 calls (101 to 200): ₹ 1.00 per call
  - For calls beyond 200: ₹ 1.50 per call
  - A fixed monthly rental charge of ₹ 125 is added to all bills.
- `void display()` : To print the bill details (`bno`, `name`, `calls`, and total `amount`).

Write a `main` method to instantiate the object using the parameterized constructor, call the `calculate()` method, and display the bill using the `display()` method.</div>
